Subsample long folders at random before stacking

A 993-frame session is not worth stacking whole. Noise falls as the square
root of the frame count, so 993 identical frames gain about 2x over 200 while
costing five times the disk (144 GB of cache) and the time. On a real session
the difference is invisible: measured side by side at 1:1, the best and worst
frames of a burst are indistinguishable, and the sharpness score that ranked
them was mostly ranking sensor noise.

New settings:

- `sample_enabled` (default on)
- `sample_threshold` (default 100): above this many files, sample
- `sample_count` (default 200): how many to keep

The draw is deterministic, seeded from the source path and the file count, so
re-running a session selects the same frames and reuses the cache instead of
building a different one. The sample is returned in capture order, because
blockwise stacking needs consecutive groups. It is a partial Fisher-Yates
shuffle over an index list: unbiased and O(n).

The pipeline emits a `Notice::Subsampled` event, which the UI shows in the
info banner ("Folder of 993 frames: 200 chosen at random"), and the report
records `sampled_from` so a small stack on a large folder is traceable.

Test: the draw is stable for a seed, returns the exact count, stays sorted,
spreads across the whole folder, and changes with the seed.
